# enemies.rec: every monster, one record each. Order is content: D1 refers to monsters by their position here.
# One schema serves every mod; a mod fills in what its game uses.
# Identity and look:
#   internal_name     id (D1's MT_* name, or "rat"); required
#   name              shown name
#   icon              icons/actors.rec id, e.g. monster.nzombie
#   tint              "R G B" its icon is tinted with (variants of one family share an icon); empty: none
#   ascii / unicode / cp437   the glyph in the text renderers
#   color             "R G B", the glyph's colour
#   graphics_family   D1's graphics folder (variants share it; some D1 rules read it)
#   speed_move        AUT a step takes (10 = one normal turn); default 10
#   speed_attack      AUT an attack takes, divided by an equipped weapon's weapon_speed; default 10
#   speed_recovery    AUT a hard hit costs it (D1: hit animation minus the attack after the blow); 0 = none
#   special_one_in    the Fallen's howl: 1 in N per turn it stands around
#   flee_distance     how far it runs when a comrade dies within 5 cells (AI_FALLEN's fear); 0: it stands
# Where it spawns (both mods: a dungeon level at depth d rolls every record with dlvl <= d <= max_dlvl; a side level's
# monster lines in levels.rec override that):
#   dlvl              the shallowest dungeon level it spawns on; max_dlvl the deepest (0: no limit)
#   monster_level     its level: to-hit against the hero, spell hit chance, experience, drops, flinching, regen;
#                     defaults to dlvl
#   random_spawn      false: never rolled at random, only placed by quests, rooms, side levels or group_spawn (default true)
#   spawn_cost        what the type costs of a level's 4000 spawn budget (D1: its graphics' memory); a level
#                     draws its monster types at random until the budget is spent, then picks among them evenly;
#                     0 (default): free, always in. A cheap type makes room for more types, a dear one crowds them out
#   wander_chance     a loner: never in the type draw; a level its depth allows gets a single one at these odds
#                     (H&H's dungen levels only; side levels roll their monster lines alone)
#   group_spawn       companions spawned with it: "<internal_name>" or "<internal_name>(<count or min-max>)"; repeatable
# Combat:
#   health            hit points: a range "4-7" (a monster with an ai rolls it and halves it, as D1's single player does), dice "1d3+1" or
#                     a number; a monster without an ai takes it as stated
#   damage / damage2  its first and second attack's damage, same notation
#   hit / hit2        to-hit of each attack (without an ai: defaults to 5 x stats_dexterity)
#   magic_res / magic_res2   resistance and immunity bits (D1's mMagicRes: IMUNE_/RESIST_ magic, fire, lightning)
#   drops             "none": never drops anything; a unique item's id ("the_undead_crown", its name as a slug):
#                     always drops it;
#                     unset: the usual roll
#   stats_<stat>      stats_armor_class and stats_experience are its AC and experience; stats_strength /
#                     _dexterity / _intelligence feed the skill-driven combat of a monster without an ai
# Behaviour:
#   ai                a D1 brain (AI_ZOMBIE, AI_SKELSD, ...; see monsters.rec); empty: skills and aggro_range drive it
#   class             MC_UNDEAD, MC_DEMON or MC_ANIMAL (hero.rec's weapon bonuses and some spells read it)
#   ai_level          0-3, how sharp its D1 ai plays (D1's mInt): more attacks and shots, shorter pauses, reacts
#                     from farther away
#   aggro_range       how far it notices the hero, for a monster without an ai
#   tags              "a | b": unique (never rolled at random, it lives in its room), no_corpse, flying,
#                     hidden (invisible), knockback (its melee pushes the hero back), searches (hunts the hero
#                     down from afar), opens_doors
#   targeted_skill / untargeted_skill   skills from effects/*.rec it uses; repeatable
#   equipment         an item it carries, by internal_name; repeatable
# Words:
#   speech_on_sight / speech_on_hurt   lines it may say on first sight / when hurt; repeatable
#   death_string      a death message format (%s is its name); repeatable
#
# Heaven&Hell's monsters in the unified enemies.rec schema every mod uses (see defs.Monster),
# ported from the game's own enemies/enemies.rec and enemies/unique_enemies.rec. The uniques are
# the records tagged "unique"; that tag is what told them apart on the old branch too.
# Combat stats follow D1's median monster at the same depth (floor L plays like D1's dlvl L; the act-two branch,
# Dungeon of Doom and Merchant District, sits at depth 17-20 past Hell):
# health ~ 6D-5 (D1's single-player hp), damage ~ 2+1.3D, hit ~ 5+8D, armor class ~ 4+5D, by monster_level;
# fodder, archers and casters get less, elites and uniques more.
# Every other record's spawn_cost is 600 + 50 x its dlvl on the old 12-floor ladder, of the 4000 budget.
#
# Converged from the old H&H field names: resource_health -> health, damage_dice -> damage,
# max_level -> max_dlvl, and the numeric icon -> an icon ID this mod defines in icons/actors.rec.

